@@ -0,0 +1,102 @@
+# NetGuardViz – Test Plan
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **System under test** | NetGuardViz: FastAPI backend, local Scapy capture agents (network + Wi-Fi/802.11), React dashboard |
+| **Status** | Baseline executed 2026-11-13 – all automated tests pass, 2 known bugs tracked (see §7) |
+| **Run it** | `pip install -r requirements-dev.txt` then `pytest` (Python 3.11 or 3.12) |
+
+## 1. Objective and scope
+
+Show, with repeatable evidence, that NetGuardViz detects what it claims to detect, keeps users' data separate, rejects bad input, and keeps working when its dependencies (AbuseIPDB, MongoDB, SMTP) misbehave.
+
+| In scope | Out of scope (and why) |
+|---|---|
+| Detectors: port scan, ARP spoofing, cleartext credentials, Wi-Fi (deauth/disassoc flood, beacon flood, evil twin, weak security, WPA handshake) | Live monitor-mode capture – needs a compatible adapter; covered by manual test MT-1 |
+| Capture pipeline: packet → detector → SIEM → database | The real AbuseIPDB service and MongoDB Atlas – replaced by test doubles so runs are fast, free and deterministic |
+| SIEM engine: normalisation, severity summary, correlation | Performance / load testing |
+| Auth: signup, login, JWT, roles, admin user management | Browser-level UI testing – covered by manual tests MT-3/MT-4; CI only checks that the frontend builds |
+| Log upload validation, keyword scan, listing, soft delete, stats | Penetration testing of the deployed site |
+
+## 2. Approach
+
+Risk-based: the most test effort goes where a failure would hurt most – **access control, detection correctness, and input validation** – and the least into cosmetic code.
+
+| Level | What it checks | How | Where |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **Unit** | One class/function in isolation (thresholds, windows, cooldowns, validators, JWT, hashing) | pytest, plain objects, scripted Scapy packets | `tests/test_detectors.py`, `test_threat_intel.py`, `test_security.py`, `test_wifi_guard.py`, validators in `test_logs_api.py` |
+| **Integration** | HTTP API + auth + database together; capture pipeline into the SIEM | FastAPI `TestClient`, `mongomock` (in-memory MongoDB), stubbed AbuseIPDB/SMTP | `test_auth_api.py`, `test_logs_api.py`, `test_alerts_api.py`, `test_alert_engine.py`, `test_capture_pipeline.py`, `test_app.py` |
+| **System / smoke** | The Wi-Fi CLI end to end on real `.pcap` files | `python -m app.wifi_capture --pcap …` with output assertions | `test_wifi_capture.py`, plus two smoke steps in CI |
+| **Static / build** | Frontend compiles; lint report | `npm run build`, `npm run lint` | CI `frontend` job |
+| **Manual** | Hardware, deployed site, browser rendering | Scripted steps (§6) | – |
+
+**Techniques used:** boundary-value analysis (abuse score 25/26/75/76, upload size 5 MB ± 1 byte, deauth threshold), equivalence classes (allowed vs. rejected extensions/MIME types), negative testing (forged, expired and `alg:none` JWTs), state-based tests (cooldown re-arming, ARP binding after a spoof), role-based access matrices (anonymous / user / admin / other user), golden-file tests (committed sample captures), and characterisation tests that pin down known gaps.
+
+## 3. Environment and test data
+
+- **Runtime:** Python 3.11 (matches `runtime.txt`) and 3.12; Node 22 for the frontend build.
+- **Hermetic by design:** `tests/conftest.py` forces test environment variables, swaps `pymongo.MongoClient` for `mongomock`, and empties the database before every test. No `.env`, API key, network or Wi-Fi hardware is needed – a developer's real `.env` cannot leak into a run.
+- **Data:** synthetic 802.11 traffic from `scripts/make_sample_pcap.py` (`samples/sample.pcap` = attack scenario, `samples/benign.pcap` = normal traffic); Scapy-built TCP/ARP packets for the network pipeline.
+- **CI:** `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on every pull request and push to `main`: backend suite on 3.11 and 3.12 with a coverage gate (≥ 85 %), two Wi-Fi smoke tests, and the frontend lint + build.
+
+## 4. Traceability
+
+| Requirement / risk | Tests | Cases |
+|---|---|---|
+| Port-scan, ARP-spoof and cleartext-credential detection work, without alert floods | `test_detectors.py`, `test_capture_pipeline.py` | 29 |
+| Malicious-IP lookups: scoring boundaries, caching, API failure handling | `test_threat_intel.py`, login tests in `test_auth_api.py` | 11 + 6 |
+| Wi-Fi attacks detected; normal Wi-Fi traffic is not flagged; sample files stay valid | `test_wifi_guard.py`, `test_wifi_capture.py` | 31 |
+| SIEM: severity counts, ordering, multi-vector correlation (IP and MAC) | `test_alert_engine.py`, `test_alerts_api.py` | 11 + 17 |
+| Authentication: forged/expired/unsigned tokens rejected, inactive users locked out at once | `test_security.py`, `test_auth_api.py` | 12 + 31 |
+| Authorisation: role comes from the database, not the token; users only see their own data | `test_auth_api.py`, `test_logs_api.py`, `test_alerts_api.py` | included above |
+| Upload hardening: extension, MIME type, size, encoding, binary content, minimum content | `test_logs_api.py` | 54 |
+| Resilience: one bad packet / failed lookup / threat-intel outage must not stop the sniffer or block login | `test_capture_pipeline.py`, `test_auth_api.py`, `test_wifi_capture.py` | included above |
+| Browser access control (CORS) and e-mail notification | `test_app.py` | 8 |
+
+**Total: 204 automated cases** (`pytest --collect-only`).
+
+## 5. Entry and exit criteria
+
+- **Entry:** code builds; dependencies install from `requirements-dev.txt`.
+- **Exit / merge gate:** all tests pass on Python 3.11 **and** 3.12 (an `xfail` is allowed only for a finding listed in §7); coverage ≥ 85 %; frontend builds; no open **High** finding.
+- **`xfail(strict=True)`** is used for known bugs: the test records the *correct* behaviour, so it turns red (XPASS) the moment the bug is fixed, which reminds us to remove the marker.
+
+## 6. Manual test cases (not automated)
+
+| ID | Test | Steps | Expected result |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **MT-1** | Live deauth-flood detection *(own lab only)* | Linux + monitor-mode adapter. `sudo venv/bin/python -m app.wifi_capture --iface wlan0mon --duration 60`. From a second machine, send a burst of deauthentication frames at **your own** test client/AP with a standard 802.11 test tool. | One `DEAUTH_FLOOD` alert naming the right BSSID and target; no alert while the network is idle. |
+| **MT-2** | Cross-check against Wireshark | Capture the same period in Wireshark; filter `wlan.fc.type_subtype == 0x0c`; run the tool on the saved pcap with `--json inv.json`. | Deauth count in Wireshark equals `frames["mgmt/12"]` in the JSON. |
+| **MT-3** | Alerts page rendering | Run the agent (without `--dry-run`) on `samples/sample.pcap`; log in as admin; open **Alerts**. Repeat as a normal user. | Admin sees 4 Wi-Fi cards with correct severity colours and MAC; keyword-log cards unchanged; normal user sees none of them; no browser-console errors. |
+| **MT-4** | Deployment smoke test | On the Vercel site: sign up, log in, upload a log containing "failed", open Alerts, open Render `/docs`. | Every step succeeds; the uploaded log produces one alert. |
+
+## 7. Findings from test design and execution
+
+| ID | Severity | Finding | Evidence | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **F1** | **High** | `.env.example` (public repo) contains a value in the format of a real AbuseIPDB API key. | File inspection | Replaced with a placeholder on this branch. **The key remains in git history – revoke/rotate it at AbuseIPDB.** |
+| **F2** | Medium | `PortScanDetector` suppresses the first alert when timestamps are smaller than the cooldown (`last_alert` defaults to 0) – affects replaying captures with relative timestamps. | `xfail` test in `test_detectors.py` | Open |
+| **F3** | Medium | `capture.process_packet()` resets `packet_count` to 0 on every packet, so the Ctrl+C throughput summary is always 0. | `xfail` test in `test_capture_pipeline.py` | Open |
+| **F4** | Medium | AbuseIPDB error responses (bad key, rate limit) are reported as "clean" – the reputation check fails open. | Characterisation test in `test_threat_intel.py` | Open |
+| **F5** | Low | Login IP check trusts the first `X-Forwarded-For` value, which a client can set to a private address to skip the lookup. | Code review (parsing is unit-tested) | Open |
+| **F6** | Low | `POST /api/threats/seed` is open to any logged-in user, so a normal user can inject alerts into the SIEM. | Characterisation test in `test_alerts_api.py` | Open – decide if intended |
+| **F7** | Low | `JWTService` falls back to the guessable secret `dev-secret-change-me` if `JWT_SECRET` is unset. | Code review | Open |
+| **F8** | Info | `test_pipeline.py` and `test_siem_engine.py` in the repo root call `AlertEngine(export_path=…)`, which no longer exists; all four root scripts need a database or network at import time. `pytest.ini` restricts collection to `tests/`. | Ran them | Open |
+| **F9** | Info | Frontend lint reports 10 existing problems (7 errors); CI lint is informational until fixed. | `npm run lint` on `main` | Open |
+| **F10** | Info | Apparently unused code: `app/auth/routes.py`, `app/models/user_models.py`, `app/models/alert_model.py`, `services/log_processor.py`, `app/utils/detector.py` (only used by the previous). | Import search; 0 % coverage | Open |
+
+## 8. Results (baseline, 2026-11-13)
+
+| Check | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Automated tests – Python 3.11 | **202 passed**, 2 expected failures (F2, F3) |
+| Automated tests – Python 3.12 | **202 passed**, 2 expected failures (F2, F3) |
+| Statement coverage (`app/` + `services/`) | **90 %** (gate: 85 %). Uncovered: live sniffing loops (MT-1), unused modules (F10) |
+| Fault-injection check | 14 deliberate bugs planted in the application code (inverted role check, off-by-one threshold, signature verification disabled, upload limit raised, …): **14 / 14 caught** by the suite |
+| Wi-Fi CLI smoke tests | Attack capture → 4 alerts as expected; benign capture → no serious alerts |
+| Workflow file | Passes `actionlint`; its steps were executed locally as written |
+| Manual tests MT-1 … MT-4 | **Not yet executed** – record results here when run |
+
+## 9. Maintenance
+
+Add a test with every bug fix (reproduce first, then fix). When a finding in §7 is fixed, delete its `xfail` marker or flip its characterisation test, and mark the finding closed.
